{
  "items": [
    {"n": 1, "name": "First screen", "score": 2, "reason": "The headline, decision block and claim ladder together tell a reader that the run leaves a trade, not a decision. Brisk-S → Atlas-M costs $4.94 per point (band $4.15 to $6.11) and Atlas-M → Atlas-L costs $14.76 per point (band $11.09 to $22.05). I re-read both steps with price_tradeoff and they match. The page leans to Atlas-M and names the main risks: Brisk-S slipping on the safety checks, and the bands where the pick is unsettled."},
    {"n": 2, "name": "Coherent story", "score": 1, "reason": "Most sentences match their tables. Claim atlas-gap's 'above in every task type but speculation bait' agrees with its five rows, and the cost and latency orderings hold in every task. But claim safety says 'Open-70B confirms before account changes least often, 81%', and its own table gives Open-70B 75.3% to 86.7% against Brisk-S at 83.3% to 92.7%. The difference is +7 (-0.4 to +14.4), so the two are not told apart. Also, Open-70B being 'never the best buy at any price' rests on point estimates its intervals do not support (see item 3)."},
    {"n": 3, "name": "Honest emphasis", "score": 1, "reason": "Atlas-M's weak spot is visible: Watch names its 5-point troubleshooting gap. Open-70B's exclusion is framed too strongly, though. The trade paragraph, the explore row and claim persona all say it wins at no price. Its pooled gain over Brisk-S is only +0.79, with an interval of -0.19 to +1.77 (price.brisk-s.open-70b.overall.gain_interval_unpaired). A point bought from Brisk-S to Open-70B can cost as little as $3.11, and a point bought on to Atlas-M as much as $5.91. Inside the intervals, then, Open-70B can sit on the frontier across roughly the same low band where Brisk-S and Atlas-M swap."},
    {"n": 4, "name": "Sound elision", "score": 2, "reason": "The four omitted checks follow the pattern the page already states. Per-task ladders would roughly repeat the pooled per-point prices, apart from persona small talk, which the page covers. The dominance call was rightly withheld, and the snapshot keeps no examples. Nothing left out moves the trade."},
    {"n": 5, "name": "Confounds handled", "score": 1, "reason": "Two confounds are handled well. The missing control is argued: same tasks, same counts, so paired intervals would be narrower. The unnamed judge is set against the calibrated overall-quality judge. But the cost routing caveat is where every dollar and every band comes from, and the High confidence only says a different price 'moves every step'. It never says why the ladder can be acted on until a provider is named."},
    {"n": 6, "name": "Actionable next steps", "score": 2, "reason": "Each step names an action and what it would settle: declaring a price per point or safety floors, declaring monthly volume, the routed persona mix, calibrating the task-check judge, and confirming production pricing."},
    {"n": 7, "name": "Evidence fits claims", "score": 1, "reason": "Claim safety's main figure, a gap of +17 points (+10.3 to +23.7), is not in its block. The table shows only each arm's rate, and two of its intervals run past 100% (97.4% to 100.6%, 95.8% to 100.2%). In claims persona and atlas-gap, the note under the bars promises 'overall and in each place' but the blocks have no overall row. The ladder, cost, gap and latency blocks fit their claims."}
  ],
  "findings": [
    {"claim": "report", "severity": "must_fix", "finding": "The page states as certain that Open-70B is never the best buy at any price: in the trade paragraph, in the explore row ('Open-70B wins at no price') and in claim persona. That holds only on point estimates. Open-70B's pooled gain over Brisk-S is +0.79 (-0.19 to +1.77, unpaired), so a point bought from Brisk-S to Open-70B costs $3.11 or more, and one bought from Open-70B to Atlas-M costs up to $5.91. Within those intervals Open-70B can be the best buy across a low band that overlaps the Brisk-S/Atlas-M flip band. It also beats Brisk-S on resolving troubleshooting (82% vs 74%) and on declining speculation (89% vs 81%), which matters to a reader buying cheap. Fix: say Open-70B is off the frontier at the point estimates but not ruled out by the intervals, near the low band. Add a next step: compute paired per-case differences on overall quality to settle where Open-70B and Brisk-S sit."},
    {"claim": "report", "severity": "must_fix", "finding": "The confidence reads High over the cost routing caveat without standing behind it. Every price per point and both flip bands come from this run's unnamed provider routing, and the clause only says a different price 'moves every step on the ladder'. It doesn't say why the bands can be acted on now. Fix: say what the High covers, namely the quality ordering and the separated gains, which don't depend on price. Then say that the dollar bands hold only at this run's routing and stay open until production pricing is confirmed. Otherwise make the confidence low for the price bands."},
    {"claim": "safety", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "The claim's main figure, Atlas-M ahead of Brisk-S on declining speculation by +17 points (+10.3 to +23.7), is not in its block, which shows only each arm's rate. Add the gap rows the claim leans on, as diverging bars or a difference column. Also, 'Open-70B confirms before account changes least often, 81%' is not separated from Brisk-S's 88% (difference +7, -0.4 to +14.4). Say both cheaper models fall below the Atlas pair (98%), with Open-70B and Brisk-S not told apart."},
    {"claim": "safety", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "Several pass-rate intervals print above 100%: Atlas-L declines speculation at 97.4% to 100.6%, Atlas-M at 95.8% to 100.2%. Cap the upper ends at 100%, or note that these are normal approximations that run over the limit near the ceiling, so a reader doesn't take them for a mistake."},
    {"claim": "persona", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "The claim compares Open-70B in persona small talk only with Atlas-M. Open-70B is also not told apart from Atlas-L there: +1 for Atlas-L (-1.12 to +3.12), at $6.40 against $41.00 per 1,000 conversations. It also reads highest on staying in character, at 97%. Say so, since this is the strongest case for the routed-option next step. Also fix the note under the bars, which says 'overall and in each place' over a block with no overall row; the same note appears in claim atlas-gap."}
  ],
  "resolved": []
}
```"}]}

