    {"n": 1, "name": "First screen", "score": 2, "reason": "The headline, decision block and claim ladder give the trade on their own. Brisk-S to Atlas-M costs $4.94 a point and Atlas-M to Atlas-L costs $14.76 a point, with flip bands of $4.15 to $6.11 and $11.09 to $22.05. The owner has to declare a price per point, and Atlas-M is a labelled lean. The main risks are on the first screen too: the provider routing is unknown, and Brisk-S slips on the safety checks."},
    {"n": 2, "name": "Coherent story", "score": 2, "reason": "Each sentence matches its values table row by row. Claim atlas-gap: Atlas-L is ahead in four task types and not told apart in speculation bait (+1, -1.546 to +3.546). Claim open-70b: above Brisk-S in troubleshooting, persona and speculation bait, below in billing, with research not separated. Claim safety: the Atlas-M gap on declining speculation is +17 (+10.3 to +23.7), and Brisk-S and Open-70B are not told apart on confirming (-0.4 to +14.4). Claim cost: troubleshooting is the dearest task for every model. Claim latency: Brisk-S has the shortest p95 in every task. Nothing contradicts the headline."},
    {"n": 3, "name": "Honest emphasis", "score": 2, "reason": "Atlas-M's weak axis is visible: Watch names its widest gap to Atlas-L, 5 points in troubleshooting. Open-70B is now called off the frontier only on the point estimates, with the interval window ($3.11 to $5.91) shown. The confidence now limits the High to the quality order and separated gains. It leaves the dollar bands open until production pricing is confirmed. Every cost on the page is flagged as this run's unnamed routing."},
    {"n": 4, "name": "Sound elision", "score": 1, "reason": "The reason given for leaving out per-task price ladders is wrong. It says persona is the only task where Open-70B moves onto the frontier. On point estimates Open-70B is also on the frontier in troubleshooting (Brisk-S to Open-70B $2.50 a point, then $7.60 on to Atlas-M) and in speculation bait ($1.13, then $4.00). In persona it beats Atlas-M outright (90 vs 88 at $6.40 vs $17.00). This does not move the pooled call, but it bears on the routed-option next step and on a low-price buyer. The other omissions hold: Open-70B's leads over Brisk-S on resolving and declining are not separated on unpaired intervals, and no failure examples are kept."},
    {"n": 5, "name": "Confounds handled", "score": 2, "reason": "Four confounds are each carried into the story and into a next step. Unknown routing leads to confirming production pricing. The undescribed task-check judge leads to naming and calibrating it. The missing control leads to a paired re-read. Best-of-several is explained as favouring the cheaper options. The overall-quality judge is calibrated (200 ratings, 86% agreement), and the page says so."},
    {"n": 6, "name": "Actionable next steps", "score": 2, "reason": "Each step names an action and what it settles: declaring a price per point or safety floors; confirming production pricing; a paired per-case re-read to place Open-70B against Brisk-S; declaring a monthly volume; running the routed mix on the same cases; calibrating the task-check judge."},
    {"n": 7, "name": "Evidence fits claims", "score": 1, "reason": "Three blocks leave out the figures their claims lean on. Claim open-70b rests on pooled figures: the gain over Brisk-S (-0.1893 to +1.769) and the price ends $3.11 and $5.91. Its block draws per-task rows only, and its caption says no overall row is drawn. Claim persona cites Atlas-L ahead by 1 (-1.121 to +3.121), Open-70B's 97% in character, and $6.40 vs $41.00. Its block shows only Open-70B less Atlas-M, in every task. Claim ladder's 5.13 and 2.96 point gains are not in its table. The safety and atlas-gap blocks now fit their claims."}
  ],
  "findings": [
    {"claim": "open-70b", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "The claim's lead sentence rests on pooled figures its block does not draw. Those are Open-70B's pooled gain over Brisk-S, +0.79 (-0.1893 to +1.769), and the price ends $3.11 (Brisk-S to Open-70B) and $5.91 (Open-70B to Atlas-M). The block shows per-task differences only, so if the pooled figures moved, nothing on the block would change. Fix: add the pooled gain as a row, or a small table of the two pooled steps with their per-point price and unpaired interval. Also reword the explore row 'it could be anywhere from $3.11 to $5.91' to say it could be the best buy at a price per point somewhere in that range.", "continues": "new"},
    {"claim": "persona", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "The claim's figures are not in its block. Those are Atlas-L ahead of Open-70B by 1 (-1.121 to +3.121), Open-70B's 97% on staying in character, and $6.40 vs $41.00 per 1,000 conversations. The block draws Open-70B less Atlas-M in all five task types, which is mostly about other tasks. Fix: draw persona small talk only, with Open-70B against both Atlas models, plus the in-character rates and the persona costs. Or narrow the sentence to what the block shows.", "continues": "new"},
    {"claim": "report", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "The page says persona is the only task where Open-70B joins the frontier, and gives that as the reason for leaving out per-task price ladders. That is wrong on the point estimates. Open-70B is also on the frontier in troubleshooting (83 vs Brisk-S 79 at $21 vs $11: $2.50 a point, then $7.60 a point on to Atlas-M) and in speculation bait ($1.13 a point, then $4.00). In persona it beats Atlas-M outright (90 vs 88, $6.40 vs $17.00). Fix: correct the reason. Then either add a sentence to claim open-70b that it sits on the per-task frontier in three of five task types, or widen the routed-option next step to say which mix it would test.", "continues": "new"},
    {"claim": "ladder", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "The claim states the gains bought at each step, 5.13 points (Brisk-S to Atlas-M) and 2.96 (Atlas-M to Atlas-L). The lede calls both separated. The table shows only the price per point. Add a gain column with its unpaired interval (+4.151 to +6.109 and +1.981 to +3.939), so a reader sees the separated gains the High stands on.", "continues": "new"}
  ],
